g_hold='\n\n'.join([
holding('Financial leave and the complete current paper',
"Gaydos's current sworn financial showing supports leave to proceed without prepayment, and the separate, documented review-access contention in her complete paper defeats the proposed Rule 39.8 denial. Financial leave does not establish entitlement to extraordinary relief, dispense with ordinary form, service or timeliness rules, or determine the separate certiorari submission.",
f"Part I of the per curiam opinion, joined by {ALL}; nine Justices adopt this record-specific application.",
"The October 8 declaration reports no employment, $6,480 annual pension and alimony, approximately $300 in cash and accounts, a car worth about $200, and no dependents. Nothing in this record contradicts that present showing. Shieh requires assessment of actual means and permits particular clarification; it does not make affidavits irrebuttable. Martin, Jones and Shieh separately require assessment of the complete current paper. Although the demanded FOIA docketing has occurred and the original-jurisdiction request fails, the October supplement raises a different, arguable question: the applicant says ordinary review was obstructed by a demand for an additional opinion that does not exist. The February reasoned letter order and the July and September Clerk correspondence give that contention independent substance. Whether it warrants a writ is a different question. Rule 39.8 permits a supported denial; it does not compel denial merely because some requested relief is unavailable. No independent finding of malice supports denial here.",
"Rule 39.1 requires the motion to state whether leave was sought in another court and whether it was granted, together with the Form 4 notarized affidavit or section 1746 declaration. When a United States district court or court of appeals has appointed counsel under 18 U.S.C. §3006A or another applicable federal statute, no affidavit or declaration is required, but the motion must cite the appointment statute; no such appointment is established here. Neither misconduct nor timely correction of every deficiency is found."),
holding('Extraordinary and original relief',
"The requested writ and alternative original action are denied: completed docketing cannot be compelled again, and this record establishes neither a proper original-jurisdiction category nor a clear and indisputable entitlement to the remaining commands with no adequate ordinary avenue. The denial leaves ordinary clarification or correction of the distinct certiorari submission available under the existing Rules without determining its timeliness, adequacy or ultimate treatment.",
f"Part II of the per curiam opinion, joined by {ASSOC}; eight Justices adopt the reasons. The Chief Justice agrees with this disposition without joining Part II.",
"The District Clerk docketed the FOIA complaint on September 9, and the complaint was dismissed the following week. Mandamus cannot compel the performed act or replace an appeal from the dismissal. A personal FOIA suit supplies no basis for this Court's original jurisdiction, and general accusations against clerks establish neither concrete disqualification nor a right to summonses here. The supplement's distinct access contention does not establish a right to require a new lower-court opinion. Rules 14.1(i) and 20.3 call for existing entered orders, opinions and essential material; they do not direct a lower court to write another opinion. The existing reasoned letter and an explanation that no additional opinion exists may be tendered through ordinary filing procedures. Blodgett applies Rule 20's requirement that adequate ordinary avenues be unavailable: this record does not establish that clarification of the stated deficiency or appropriate lower-court relief is unavailable or inadequate. Arguable access concerns therefore support financial leave without satisfying the more demanding prerequisites for extraordinary relief.",
"No new filing is commanded, no acceptance order issues, no correction is declared untimely, and no new deadline or default is imposed. The Court decides no FOIA merits, clerk misconduct or separate certiorari outcome."),
holding('Prospective fee and form conditions',
"No prospective condition issues for either noncriminal certiorari submissions or extraordinary-writ submissions. Such a condition requires a supported showing tied to this applicant, the relevant filing category, and the inadequacy of treating particular papers; the present record does not supply that showing for either category.",
f"Part III-A of the per curiam opinion, addressing extraordinary-writ submissions, is joined by {ALL}. Part III-B, addressing noncriminal certiorari submissions, is joined by {ASSOC}; the Chief Justice agrees with its disposition without joining its reasoning. Each portion independently commands a majority.",
"Martin and Shieh preserve separate inquiries into a present paper and a future category. The actual current paper and the record before the Court do not establish a category-specific sequence showing that individual treatment will be inadequate. Filing totals or historical descriptions cannot supply the missing connection. Preserving a paid route and unrelated criminal access would narrow a restriction, but would not justify it. McDonald and Sindram retain authority for targeted protection against demonstrated extraordinary-writ abuse; neither establishes this applicant's predicates. The Court declines both proposed conditions on the present showing.",
"No universal warning requirement, numerical threshold, exhaustion of every conceivable lesser measure, or categorical prohibition of later supported restrictions is adopted.")])
g_prec="""- Martin v. District of Columbia Court of Appeals and Martin v. McDermott (November 2, 1992): applies the distinct current-paper and prospective-condition inquiries; a defective request and filing history do not answer an independent arguable access contention.
- Jones v. ABC-TV (February 26, 1996): applies separate financial, present-content and future-category analysis; no financial default is inferred from incomplete historical descriptions.
- Shieh v. Kakita and companions (April 1, 1996): applies actual-means assessment, separate consideration of concrete access content, and the applicant/category/inadequacy requirement.
- Zatko v. California (November 4, 1991): preserves discretionary denial supported by particular-paper frivolousness or malice; no such complete-paper finding is made here.
- In re Blodgett (January 13, 1992): applies the adequate-ordinary-avenue prerequisite to extraordinary relief without deciding the underlying merits.
- In re McDonald, 489 U.S. 180, and In re Sindram, 498 U.S. 177: retain targeted process-protection authority on supported records; no new future restriction follows here."""
