m_hold='\n\n'.join([
holding('No live geographic placement controversy',
"The superseded placement claim is moot because the November 1993 individualized education program supplies the Olathe placement sought and no redressable residue or reasonable expectation of the same challenged transfer recurring as to Tyler is established. Recurring annual IEP reviews and an enduring disagreement about statutory meaning do not alone satisfy the exception for an injury capable of repetition yet too short for full review.",
f"Part I of Scalia's opinion of the Court, adopted by {ASSOC}; eight Justices adopt this threshold ground. The Chief Justice dissents.",
"The later IEP affirmatively places Tyler at Olathe. No continuing transfer directive, damages, reimbursement demand or other remedial interest in the old placement is supplied. Honig requires a reasonable expectation of recurrence as to the same claimant as well as a duration too short for complete litigation. An annual placement process may present the latter concern, but cannot establish the former. Honig's surviving claimant faced a supported prospect of repeated exclusion; this record supplies no comparable renewed geographic threat. Northeastern Florida preserved review because the successor rule maintained the concrete challenged barrier. Here the subsequent placement removed it. The Court cannot decide the proposed neighborhood-school presumption in the abstract or revive an obsolete remedy by receiving more evidence. Granting review does not dispense with a live controversy.",
"The Court establishes no categorical rule that every replacement IEP moots every IDEA case. It decides no neighborhood-school presumption, specialized-school legality, educational adequacy, supplementary-aid exhaustion, or general additional-evidence rule."),
holding('Equitable disposition of the obsolete merits judgments',
"The Tenth Circuit's merits affirmance is vacated, and the case is remanded with instructions to vacate the district court's merits judgment on the superseded placement claim and dismiss that claim as moot. The separate cross-appeal dismissal remains undisturbed because no live additional relief is available on the obsolete placement controversy, independently of the now-vacated district-court merits victory.",
f"Part II of Scalia's opinion, adopted by {ASSOC}; eight Justices adopt this equitable remedy under 28 U.S.C. §2106.",
"Bancorp distinguishes the loss of authority to decide the merits from the authority to dispose appropriately of the judgments under review. Vacatur is equitable, not automatic. Here an ordinary later educational reassessment supplied the requested placement after the district judgment while the parents continued to seek review. No settlement or voluntary abandonment of review by the losing parties is shown. The unreviewable merits judgments concern that superseded placement. Munsingwear therefore supports removing them within the stated bounds, rather than preserving an abstract merits adjudication or merely dismissing this Court's review. The district's cross-appeal sought an earlier summary-judgment victory, but the obsolete placement controversy now supplies no live additional relief. Its dismissal therefore survives independently of the vacated merits judgment.",
"No unrelated administrative decision is erased. No new IEP, transfer, evidence hearing or exhaustion process is commanded, and neither side receives a merits victory.")])
m_separate="""Chief Justice Stone-Zsela dissents. He would affirm the Tenth Circuit and reach the preserved geographic legal question while treating the individualized former-placement and later-progress disputes as moot. In his view, IDEA's preference for education with nondisabled children is distinct from geographic proximity: the regulations make proximity and the neighborhood school mandatory considerations, but permit another arrangement when the individualized IEP requires it. He would reject an independent burden-shifting neighborhood-school presumption without approving convenience-based transfers, diminishing the duty to use supplementary aids, or declaring distance irrelevant. A district's assertion that its specialized program is better would not establish compliance.

He would recognize section 1415(e)(2)'s additional-evidence channel in a live dispute, including relevant subsequent progress with its temporal relevance explained, while retaining due weight for the administrative proceedings. He would order no new evidence here: the aids proffer does not establish the geographic-presumption theory, the independent unexhausted aids issue is not reached, and the later IEP removes the placement/progress remedy. He declines the wider evidentiary disagreement and any ruling on Northside's factual adequacy. His proposed legal answer and affirmance do not command a majority; the Court holds the controversy moot."""
DATA.append(dict(key='Murray',number=8,name='Tyler Murray v. Montrose County School District RE-1J',dockets='Tenth Circuit Nos. 93-1466 and 93-1474; no Supreme Court docket assigned',date='1996-12-02',kind='Threshold disposition after merits argument',result='Vacate merits affirmance and remand for limited vacatur and mootness dismissal, 8–1; cross-appeal dismissal undisturbed.',
event="""Tyler Murray v. Montrose County School District RE-1J. On writ of certiorari to the Tenth Circuit, 51 F.3d 921; lower-court Nos. 93-1466 and 93-1474. Argued October 7, 1996; decided December 2, 1996. The questions concern a neighborhood-school presumption under IDEA, use of another school to provide individualized specialized services, and evidence beyond the administrative record. The parents seek reversal and further consideration of aids, later progress and transfer appropriateness. The parents and team agreed on needs, goals and regular/special-class allocation, but disputed Olathe versus Northside. After the October 21, 1993 district judgment, the November 1993 IEP affirmatively placed Tyler at Olathe. The Tenth Circuit on April 4, 1995 affirmed for the district and dismissed its cross-appeal; rehearing was denied May 8, 1995. No separate Supreme Court companion or docket number is supplied.""",
participation=f'All nine Justices participate at argument and decision: {ALL}.',
action='The Court resolves review on mootness and the proper disposition of the judgments, without deciding the granted IDEA merits questions.',
judgment=f"""| Component | Disposition and vote | Supporting | Opposing | Remedy |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Merits affirmance concerning the superseded placement | Vacated, 8–1 | {ASSOC} | Stone-Zsela | Remand to vacate the district-court merits judgment on that claim and dismiss it as moot |
| Separate cross-appeal dismissal | Undisturbed, 9–0 | {ALL} | | No live additional relief remains on the obsolete placement controversy |""",
topology=f'Scalia delivers the opinion of the Court, Parts I and II, joined by Stevens, O\'Connor, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, Ginsburg and Breyer. Chief Justice Stone-Zsela dissents; no Justice joins his writing.',
holdings=m_hold,precedent="""- Honig v. Doe, 484 U.S. 305: applies the separate requirements of reasonable same-claimant recurrence and duration too short for full review; the recurrence record is distinguished.
- Northeastern Florida Chapter, AGC v. Jacksonville (June 14, 1993): distinguishes a replacement that maintains the actual barrier from this IEP's removal of the challenged placement.
- U.S. Bancorp Mortgage Co. v. Bonner Mall Partnership (November 8, 1994): applies the bar on moot merits adjudication and the separate equitable authority under section 2106; its voluntary-settlement setting is distinguished.
- United States v. Munsingwear, Inc., 340 U.S. 36: applies equitable disposition when ordinary review becomes unavailable; no automatic vacatur rule is adopted.
- Rowley and Florence County v. Carter supply no decision of the geographic or evidence questions because the Court does not reach those merits.""",
law='The mootness and equitable-vacatur rules are applied to a superseded school-placement dispute. This decision creates no controlling IDEA geography, mainstreaming, additional-evidence or exhaustion rule. The vacated merits judgments supply no operative merits disposition of the superseded placement claim.',
separate=m_separate,procedure='The Tenth Circuit must implement the limited vacatur and remand for dismissal of the superseded placement claim as moot. Its cross-appeal dismissal remains because that obsolete controversy offers no live additional relief, not because a district-court merits victory remains affirmed. The later IEP is not altered and no new placement adjudication is ordered.',
sources='[The Tenth Circuit opinion, 51 F.3d 921](https://openjurist.org/51/f3d/921), including its separate evidence and mootness discussions, supplies the procedural record. No later threatened transfer, damages or reimbursement claim is established. The Court relies on the affirmative later IEP and relief actually presented, not an inference that unlocated records cannot exist.',
stone="""The approved supplement fixes affirmance and a merits answer to the enduring geographic legal dispute while recognizing that the old placement and progress remedies are moot. Assembly preserves that position as a dissent. It does not silently make Stone join the eight Associates' conclusion that the whole placement controversy, including its abstract legal formulation, is moot. Nor does it invent a same-child recurrence finding or an approved Honig exception for him. He supports leaving the cross-appeal dismissal in place as part of his express affirmance. His proposed geography and evidence positions are recorded in full substance in the separate-writing summary and continuity; no missing Stone vote or fallback is required.""",
assignment="""The eight-Associate judgment coalition excludes the Chief on the controlling mootness disposition and limited vacatur. Stevens, the senior participating Associate, assigns the coordinated opinion to Scalia. Fit follows Scalia's own Honig dissent's insistence on actual recurrence and his Bancorp opinion's distinction between jurisdiction and equitable judgment disposition. Souter and Thomas provide supported alternatives through the concrete-threat and replacement-barrier cases, but Scalia's own work directly covers both dispositive questions. Stone cannot assign from this coalition; his broader affirmance is not substituted. No named Chief assignment is added. No historically attributed Supreme Court author exists for this review.""",
joins="""Every Associate joins both threshold and equitable-remedy parts at the frozen scope; the general evidence and geography questions remain untouched. Stevens's Honig-majority position is preserved by the explanation that actual supported recurrence there differs from the absent geographic threat here. The shared opinion adopts no categorical rule that a new IEP always moots review, no universal vacatur command, and no implied exhaustion reversal. Stone's merits insistence is an unresolved genuine disagreement and is preserved as dissent. No draft-history negotiation or changed Associate commitment is invented.""",
noncontrol=m_separate,
snapshot='Honig supplies the recurrence/evasion exception. The current Northeastern Florida holding requires a maintained concrete barrier; Bancorp governs section 2106 and equitable vacatur. Carter is limited to its reimbursement setting and supplies no geography rule. Rowley and the statutory evidence channel would matter only to a live merits controversy.',
uncertainty='No live post-1993 transfer or residual monetary claim is supplied. The current merits entry and October 7 argument are adopted scheduling premises. The case is resolved on the actual later placement without inventing a subsequent settlement or abandonment.'
))

j_hold=holding('Immediate review of the completed conditional enforcement judgment',
"An order completing a summons-enforcement proceeding is final under 28 U.S.C. §1291, including its presently binding notice condition. The Government's challenge to this condition is ripe before an interdivision transfer occurs because the judgment already fixes its legal obligation; it need not violate the order or await contempt to obtain review.",
f'The per curiam opinion is joined by {ALL}; all nine adopt ordinary finality and the concrete present-restraint ground.',
"The district court enforced two summonses directed to Jose as trustee of two trusts, while requiring five days' notice before circulating, transferring or copying the produced material to another IRS division, including Criminal Investigation. Jose could then file and serve a motion to prohibit the proposed action. The enforcement proceeding ended with that conclusive obligation. The Ninth Circuit expressly accepted section 1291 jurisdiction but dismissed for ripeness because no transfer had been attempted and the investigation was represented to be civil. Those facts do not make the existing judicial condition hypothetical. Removing it would give the Government effectual legal relief. Church of Scientology distinguishes meaningful appellate relief from entitlement on the enforcement merits. Digital Equipment's limits on collateral appeals are preserved: this is an ordinary appeal from a completed proceeding, not a special right arising from compliance costs. Quackenbush likewise requires the actual appellate route and conclusive effect to be identified. The Court does not hold that every final order necessarily presents a ripe controversy; this particular binding restraint does.",
"The remand concerns the notice condition's legality and preserved related enforcement-power issues. The uncontested findings that the civil-purpose summonses are valid and should be enforced are not reopened. Privacy interests and hypothetical future criminal uses are not adjudicated by this threshold ruling. Zolin's equal division supplies no controlling answer about conditions.")
DATA.append(dict(key='Jose',number=9,name='United States v. Jose',dockets='No. 95-2082',date='1996-12-02',kind='Summary appellate disposition',result='Reverse ripeness dismissal and remand for the Government\'s appeal concerning both summonses, 9–0.',
event='United States v. Jose, No. 95-2082. Summary appellate disposition on December 2, 1996; submitted without oral argument. On review of the Ninth Circuit\'s dismissal of the Government\'s appeal from a completed conditional summons-enforcement order. Two summonses concerned Jose as trustee of two trusts. The question is immediate appellate review of the five-day notice condition before internal IRS circulation, transfer or copying. The Ninth Circuit accepted section 1291 jurisdiction but dismissed for ripeness; its dissent would have reached the condition\'s merits. The United States seeks reversal and merits review; Jose invokes the absence of a concrete proposed transfer and defends the condition.',
participation=f'All nine Justices participate: {ALL}.',
action='The Ninth Circuit\'s ripeness dismissal is reversed. The case is remanded for appellate consideration of the Government\'s preserved challenge to the notice condition covering both summonses. No certiorari poll is disclosed.',
judgment=f'Reversed and remanded, 9–0. Supporting: {ALL}. Both summonses are within the remand; the Court neither invalidates the condition nor decides its ultimate merits.',
topology=f'The Court issues a per curiam opinion joined in full by {ALL}. No separate writing issues.',
holdings=j_hold,precedent="""- Church of Scientology of California v. United States (November 16, 1992): applies the distinction between effectual appellate relief and ultimate enforcement entitlement; its reservation of conditions and future-use restrictions remains.
- Digital Equipment Corp. v. Desktop Direct, Inc. (June 6, 1994): preserves the cumulative collateral-order requirements and rejects a free-standing hardship exception; this order is final in the ordinary completed-proceeding sense.
- Quackenbush v. Allstate Insurance Co. (June 3, 1996): applies source-specific appellate analysis and conclusive legal effect; no additional collateral-order category is created.
- United States v. Zolin, 491 U.S. 554: its equal division over enforcement-condition authority supplies no controlling answer to the reserved merits question.""",
law='The existing final-judgment and live-controversy rules permit immediate review of this actual conditional enforcement judgment. The holding does not expand collateral-order jurisdiction or decide the permissible scope of IRS protective conditions.',
separate='',procedure='The Government\'s appeal returns to the Ninth Circuit for the preserved merits of the notice condition. The condition is not invalidated by this threshold ruling; lawful further appellate proceedings determine its treatment.',
sources='The district-court order and [Ninth Circuit opinion, 71 F.3d 1484](https://openjurist.org/71/f3d/1484), supply the two summonses, operative notice terms, civil-purpose representation and acknowledged appellate jurisdiction. [The reported procedural recitation](https://www.law.cornell.edu/supct/html/95-2082.ZPC.html) cross-checks that record. No attempted interdivision transfer or inevitable investigative injury is found.',
stone='Stone expressly approves reversal of the ripeness dismissal and remand without deciding the condition\'s validity. The full adopted rationale—ordinary finality, present binding restraint, no need for violation or contempt, and a distinct merits inquiry—fits his approved core and Digital Equipment/Church of Scientology reservations. He joins the whole per curiam opinion. The two summonses are the operative scope of the same order, not an added unresolved substantive claim; no fallback is needed.',
assignment='The settled summary threshold disposition is expressed through an institutional per curiam. All nine share the bounded finality/ripeness ground and its reservations; no accommodation-dependent expansion or named historical author is imported. This unattributed institutional opinion creates no named Chief assignment.',
joins='Each Associate\'s frozen commitment is satisfied by separating section 1291 finality, ripeness of this present judicial restraint and the unresolved substantive condition. The actual completed proceeding answers Scalia\'s hardship-only objection and the comparable limits in every row. Stone\'s explicit grounds support the same complete opinion. No Associate position is changed and no new concern about a hypothetical future transfer is adjudicated.',
noncontrol='No separate writing or new material noncontrolling proposition issues.',
snapshot='Church of Scientology allows meaningful review but reserves conditions; Digital Equipment limits collateral appeals; Quackenbush requires the actual appellate source. Section 1291 ordinary finality and the presently operative judicial obligation govern the threshold only.',
uncertainty='No transfer has occurred. That fact limits hypothetical-application claims but does not defeat review of the existing final legal obligation. The supplied summary appellate entry does not create an additional grant poll or new proceeding.'
))

h_hold='\n\n'.join([
holding('Independent failure of traceability',
"Assuming sufficient personal aesthetic injury from Lota's absence, the Society has not shown that the challenged August 1991 ESA certificate amendment caused the lost zoo access. The independent earlier donation produced that loss, and this record establishes no mechanism connecting the later amendment to its continuation.",
f'Part I of Souter\'s opinion of the Court, adopted by {ALL}; nine Justices adopt this independently sufficient jurisdictional ground.',
"Lujan requires support for the plaintiff's own injury and its connection to the challenged action at the litigation stage presented. A person can have a concrete aesthetic interest in observing a particular animal; species-wide depletion, ownership and a scientific profession are not prerequisites. The Court nevertheless assumes actual injury here rather than finding that the hypothetical regular observer described in the question exists in the evidence. The zoo's donation preceded the amendment, which governed later uses by Hawthorn. The Society abandoned its separate appeal concerning the original transfer and did not challenge the CITES certificate. Neither action can be restored to the case by calling this a challenge to continued absence. Government permission can be causally effective through supported, predictable private conduct; a government command is not invariably necessary. But this record supplies no such link between the later amendment and the lost Milwaukee observation. Sweet Home's treatment of actual injury to individual animals under a habitat regulation does not supply the missing human standing link for this different transportation permission.",
"The Court does not decide whether the actual members' affidavits establish injury in fact. It adopts no categorical rule against third-party causation, no purchased-ticket requirement, and no general prohibition on standing to observe one animal."),
holding('Independent failure of likely beneficial relief',
"The Society also fails independently to show that suspending the challenged certificate or declaring the transportation interpretation unlawful would likely benefit a member's impaired observation. Complete restoration is unnecessary, but a regulatory change must offer some likely concrete improvement to the member's interest.",
f'Part II of the same unanimous opinion; all nine Justices independently adopt this remedial standing ground.',
"Invalidating the amendment would leave Hawthorn able to retain Lota at its Illinois premises. It would not annul the donation, compel the zoo to receive her, or make renewed Milwaukee access substantially likely. No supported alternative benefit to an established observer appears in the affidavits or requested relief. Church of Scientology permits meaningful partial relief even when full restoration is impossible, but changing another person's legal options is not beneficial relief to a member by definition. Even with favorable reasonable inferences from the existing materials and assumed injury, the missing connection remains. The Winikoff letters were considered because the objection to their unsworn form was not preserved; considering them does not supply absent facts. The Owens letter addresses the Society's access, not its authors' personal observation. A remand to create a new return mechanism or observer would not correct an established error in this record.",
"No categorical conclusion is reached about remedies in another properly supported action. The Court orders no return, certificate cancellation, damages or other substantive relief."),
holding('Procedural and representative claims retain the concrete-interest connection',
"An asserted ESA procedure does not cure the missing connection between this challenged amendment and a represented member's concrete interest. Assuming that the asserted conservation-permit procedure exists, a claimant need not prove that proper procedure certainly changes the ultimate agency decision, but must still show the threatened personal interest that the procedure protects; representative litigation does not dispense with that constitutional requirement.",
f'Part III of the same unanimous opinion; all nine Justices reject these proposed ways around the causal and remedial defects.',
"Lujan preserves procedural protection for threatened concrete interests without requiring certainty of a different final result. It does not authorize general judicial supervision based on an interest in lawful conservation administration. No individual entitlement to Lota's Milwaukee residence or separate informational right is established here. Assuming the asserted section 1539 procedure for this analysis does not convert that section's permission into a found statutory command. Brown Group preserves concrete injury, traceability and redressability for represented members; its displacement of Hunt's prudential participation limit through WARN's express authorization supplies no ESA exemption. The procedure asserted in this case does not connect the later amendment to the completed transfer or establish likely benefit to the zoo observer. The Court therefore need not construe the commercial-activity provision or the asserted permit duty.",
"Whether section 1539 actually imposes the claimed procedure, the commercial-activity interpretation, the unchallenged CITES certificate, the abandoned transfer claim and Hawthorn's Chaney objection remain undecided.")])
DATA.append(dict(key='Humane_Society',number=11,name='Humane Society v. Babbitt',dockets='D.C. Circuit No. 93-5339; District of Columbia No. 92-cv-0952; no Supreme Court docket assigned',date='1996-12-09',kind='Jurisdictional disposition after merits hearing',result='Affirm jurisdictional dismissal on independent causation and redressability failures, 9–0; actual injury assumed.',
event='Humane Society v. Babbitt. On writ of certiorari to the D.C. Circuit, 46 F.3d 93; D.C. Circuit No. 93-5339 and district-court No. 92-cv-0952. Review was granted October 7, 1996. Argued and decided December 9, 1996 under the expedited schedule. The three questions concern personal aesthetic injury from loss of access to a particular animal, causation and redressability when federal permission enables private action, and procedural standing without a separately established concrete interest. The Society challenges the August 1991 ESA amendment to Hawthorn\'s certificate and seeks suspension and a declaration against the commercial-activity interpretation. The November 1990 donation preceded that amendment; the original-transfer claim was abandoned on appeal, and the July 1991 CITES certificate was not challenged. The D.C. Circuit vacated the district court\'s statutory merits judgment and directed dismissal for lack of Article III jurisdiction. No Supreme Court docket number is supplied.',
participation=f'All nine Justices participate at argument and decision: {ALL}.',
action='The D.C. Circuit\'s jurisdictional judgment is affirmed on the independently sufficient failures of causation and redressability, assuming adequate injury in fact.',
judgment=f'Affirmed, 9–0. Supporting: {ALL}. The district court\'s statutory merits judgment remains vacated and the action is dismissed for want of Article III jurisdiction. The Court makes no ESA merits ruling.',
topology=f'Souter delivers the opinion of the Court, Parts I–III, joined in full by Stone-Zsela, Stevens, O\'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Thomas, Ginsburg and Breyer. No separate writing issues.',
holdings=h_hold,precedent="""- Lujan v. Defenders of Wildlife (June 12, 1992): applies its controlling elements, stage-specific evidence and concrete-interest/procedural-right rules while preserving reasonable inferences and cognizable personal aesthetic use. Its three-Justice absent-agency discussion is not made controlling.
- Church of Scientology of California v. United States (November 16, 1992): applies the sufficiency of meaningful partial relief; distinguishes a constraint on a third party that supplies no demonstrated benefit to the claimant.
- United Food & Commercial Workers v. Brown Group (May 13, 1996): preserves represented members' constitutional standing requirements; its WARN-specific displacement of Hunt's prudential participation condition is not extended to an ESA exemption.
- Hunt v. Washington State Apple Advertising Commission, 432 U.S. 333: retains the need for a member with constitutional standing.
- Babbitt v. Sweet Home Chapter (June 29, 1995): its actual-injury and statutory-source distinctions remain; its habitat-harm holding and separate deference rationale do not decide transportation permission or human standing here.""",
law='The Court applies constitutional causation, redressability and procedural-standing rules to this certificate challenge. Actual injury is assumed and unresolved; the discussion of observing a particular animal does not find an injured member or independently decide the suit. No ESA commercial-activity or permit-procedure construction is adopted. Jurisdictional dismissal does not decide a genuinely different claim supported by adequate standing facts and does not reopen the abandoned transfer count.',
separate='',procedure='The jurisdictional dismissal stands and the vacated statutory merits judgment is not restored. No certificate is cancelled, procedure ordered, custody transferred or damages awarded; no stay issues.',
sources='[The D.C. Circuit opinion, 46 F.3d 93](https://openjurist.org/46/f3d/93), supplies the sequence, requested relief, affidavits, treatment of the letters and alternative standing grounds. The hearing followed shortened briefing under Rule 25.4: opening November 1, response November 22 and reply December 2. That schedule establishes no disputed standing fact. No actual member is found to possess every fact assumed in the first question.',
stone='The approved supplement expressly permits assuming injury and resolves the case on missing causal and remedial links. The shared opinion recognizes concrete observation without finding a hypothetical observer, rejects a compulsion-only test without finding unsupported regulatory causation, and preserves the procedural concrete-interest rule without adopting Stone\'s broader personal-right theory. Those exact grounds and reservations support Stone\'s complete join. His nonjoin in Sweet Home\'s deference rationale does not erase that controlling prior rationale and creates no different present judgment. No fallback or added substantive premise is used.',
assignment='Stone belongs to the settled unanimous judgment and reasoning coalition and assigns the opinion to Souter. His own Brown Group opinion supplies the precise relationship between representative enforcement and members\' constitutional interests; his Lujan joins and Sweet Home opinion address concrete proof and distinct statutory sources. Thomas\'s Northeastern Florida authorship is a strong alternative for lost-opportunity causation, and Stone\'s approved position is well suited to separating injury and remedy. Souter\'s direct authorial experience connecting all three inquiries supplies the concrete fit advantage. No expanded coalition is needed and no workload-based quota controls. The preceding-term baseline is 101 named Chief assignments with no majority recipient, so no concentration ceiling is triggered. This is one named Chief assignment to Souter; the final chronological ordinal remains for root integration.',
joins='Every Associate\'s frozen H1–H3 commitment is retained. The opinion assumes the actual injury prong, distinguishes permission from compulsion without finding a causal mechanism, requires likely beneficial partial relief, and assumes rather than decides the statutory procedure. These formulations answer the recorded risks of a species-wide prerequisite, categorical third-party bar, invented observer, automatic benefit and unsupported statutory duty. Stone\'s express position supports all three parts at that same level; none of his additional personal-right vocabulary is attributed to the Associates. No new vote or altered non-Stone commitment requires rereconciliation.',
noncontrol='No separate writing or new material noncontrolling proposition issues. The assumed injury and unanswered statutory questions remain express limits of the unanimous decision.',
snapshot='Lujan supplies five-Justice controlling standing rules, not its three-Justice absent-agency analysis. Brown Group retains constitutional member standing while displacing a prudential condition only through WARN\'s authorization. Church of Scientology requires meaningful benefit; Sweet Home does not construe the transportation exemption. The full B slice contains their exact controlling scopes.',
uncertainty='The continuous-petition, October 7 grant and expedited briefing/hearing are adopted entry and scheduling premises, not reconstructed member evidence. No new affidavit, return plan, regulatory causal mechanism or enforceable section 1539 command is inferred.'
))

c_hold='\n\n'.join([
holding('The erroneous refusal to remand remains error',
"Complete diversity was absent when Caterpillar removed, and Lewis's timely motion required remand under the applicable removal and remand statutes. The later dismissal of the nondiverse party does not make the earlier ruling correct, waive Lewis's preserved objection, or require him to have sought extraordinary pretrial relief.",
f'Part I of O\'Connor\'s opinion of the Court, adopted by {ALL}; all nine adopt this preserved-error proposition.',
"Lewis and Whayne were Kentucky citizens, and Liberty's surviving subrogation claim kept Whayne in the action when Caterpillar removed on June 21, 1990. The district court should have granted the timely remand motion instead of denying it on September 24. Lewis's separate contention that his own settlement was partial need not be resolved because Liberty's claim already defeated complete diversity. Sections 1441 and 1447 retain their actual requirements; finality is no permission to disregard them. Things Remembered and Quackenbush preserve the mandatory-remand and review rules at their proper procedural stages. This case concerns refusal to remand followed by a later trial, not appellate review of an actually entered remand covered by section 1447(d).",
"No collateral challenge to an entered covered remand is authorized. No late alternative removal argument or independent fee or sanction entitlement is decided."),
holding('Actual jurisdiction before trial preserves the completed judgment against this earlier defect',
"When complete diversity actually arises through the nondiverse party's lawful dismissal before trial and persists through judgment, an earlier erroneous refusal of a timely remand motion does not by itself require vacating the completed federal judgment. The rule depends on actual section 1332 jurisdiction during trial and judgment; consent, finality or efficiency cannot cure a judgment entered without that jurisdiction.",
f'Part II of the same opinion, adopted by {ALL}; all nine adopt this bounded extension to a preserved removal objection.',
"Whayne's dismissal following the June 1993 settlement left complete diversity before the November trial and through judgment. Finn required correction where the nondiverse party remained through judgment; it does not require destruction of a judgment that possessed original jurisdiction when tried. Grubbs distinguishes a completed judgment's jurisdictional foundation from earlier removal error, but involved trial without objection. The Court expressly extends that distinction to this timely preserved objection only after the actual pretrial cure. Newman-Green recognizes that party dismissal can cure statutory diversity, subject to its separate dispensability and prejudice limits. This case does not require an appellate court to dismiss a party or manufacture jurisdiction over a completed nondiverse trial. The strongest contrary argument gives Lewis's preserved forum objection a judgment-destroying remedy. Preservation makes the earlier error reviewable, but does not dictate that consequence after the obstacle ended before merits adjudication. Willy separates independently authorized collateral consequences from merits jurisdiction; it supplies no jurisdiction and does not save an uncured trial. Existing jurisdiction and the final-judgment interest, not economy alone, sustain this limited result.",
"No universal cure for every removal defect follows. The decision authorizes no collusive manufacture of diversity, finds no manipulation, and does not extinguish a separately authorized fee or sanction request."),
holding('Restoration against jurisdictional vacatur leaves the ordinary appeal for decision',
"The Sixth Circuit's jurisdiction-based vacatur and state-court-remand direction are reversed; the district-court judgment is restored against that jurisdictional ground, subject to the unresolved ordinary appeal. The case returns to the Sixth Circuit to decide the five preserved issues it did not reach: prior-accident discovery restrictions, the discovery-sanction request, excluded exhibits, excluded witnesses and the omitted failure-to-warn instruction.",
f'Part III of O\'Connor\'s opinion, adopted by {ASSOC}; eight Justices adopt the remand explanation. The Chief Justice agrees with this remand disposition without joining Part III. All nine support reversal and the stated disposition.',
"The granted jurisdictional question does not decide the other appellate challenges to the defense verdict. Restoring a judgment against an erroneous jurisdictional vacatur does not make it immune from ordinary appellate correction. The Court therefore returns those distinct questions rather than finding the discovery, evidentiary, sanction or instruction objections meritless without deciding them. No fee, sanction or new trial is awarded here. The Sixth Circuit must decide the preserved issues under their governing law.",
"The Court does not finally affirm the defense verdict against the five remaining challenges or decide any of their merits.")])
DATA.append(dict(key='Caterpillar',number=12,name='Caterpillar Inc. v. Lewis',dockets='No. 95-1263',date='1996-12-10',kind='Merits decision',result='Reverse jurisdictional vacatur; restore district judgment against that ground and remand for five remaining appellate issues, 9–0.',
event='Caterpillar Inc. v. Lewis, No. 95-1263. On writ of certiorari to the Sixth Circuit. Argued November 12, 1996; decided December 10, 1996. The question is whether lack of complete diversity at removal requires vacating a federal judgment despite actual complete diversity before trial and through judgment where the plaintiff timely sought remand. Lewis sued Caterpillar and Whayne in Kentucky court; Liberty Mutual intervened. Removal occurred June 21, 1990, remand was denied September 24, 1990, and the Whayne/Liberty settlement occurred June 8, 1993. Whayne was dismissed before the November 15–22, 1993 trial. The jury returned a defense verdict. The Sixth Circuit vacated the federal judgment and directed state-court remand solely for the initial diversity defect, leaving five other appellate issues undecided.',
participation=f'All nine Justices participate at argument and decision: {ALL}.',
action='The Sixth Circuit\'s jurisdiction-based vacatur and state-court-remand direction are reversed. The federal judgment is restored against that ground, and the case is remanded for the unresolved ordinary appellate issues.',
judgment=f"""| Component | Disposition and vote | Supporting | Opposing | Remedy |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Jurisdiction-based vacatur and state-court-remand direction | Reversed, 9–0 | {ALL} | | Restore the district-court judgment against this jurisdictional ground |
| Five unaddressed appellate issues | Remanded for decision, 9–0 | {ALL} | | Ordinary appellate review remains; the verdict is not finally affirmed against those challenges |""",
topology=f"""| Writing | Author | Joined by | Scope and relationship |
|---|---|---|---|
| Opinion of the Court, Parts I–II | O'Connor | Stone-Zsela, Stevens, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, Ginsburg and Breyer | Earlier preserved removal error and actual pretrial jurisdiction; unanimous reasoning |
| Opinion of the Court, Part III | O'Connor | Stevens, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, Ginsburg and Breyer | Reasons for returning the five undecided appellate issues; the Chief Justice agrees with that disposition only |""",
holdings=c_hold,precedent="""- American Fire & Casualty Co. v. Finn, 341 U.S. 6: distinguishes its jurisdictional defect persisting through judgment from actual complete diversity before this trial; its prohibition on jurisdiction by consent remains.
- Grubbs v. General Electric Credit Corp., 405 U.S. 699: extends its final-judgment distinction beyond its no-objection facts only to the preserved error and actual pretrial cure here; it is not described as already deciding this case.
- Newman-Green, Inc. v. Alfonzo-Larrain, 490 U.S. 826: preserves dismissal-based diversity cure and its dispensability/prejudice limits; no appellate dismissal or retrospective creation of this trial's jurisdiction is needed.
- Willy v. Coastal Corp. (March 9, 1992): applies the separation between independently authorized collateral consequences and merits jurisdiction; its holding does not itself confer jurisdiction or validate an uncured merits judgment.
- Things Remembered, Inc. v. Petrarca (December 5, 1995), and Quackenbush v. Allstate Insurance Co. (June 3, 1996): preserve the covered-remand review bar and source/stage distinctions; no bar concerning an entered covered remand is displaced.""",
law='The earlier statutory remand duty and Lewis\'s timely objection remain valid. The Court adds a bounded rule that actual diversity before trial and through judgment prevents this preserved earlier removal error alone from destroying the completed judgment. Original-jurisdiction requirements, lawful party dismissal, covered-remand review limits and ordinary appellate remedies remain. No doctrine of universal harmless removal error or unconditional verdict affirmance results.',
separate='',procedure='The case returns to the Sixth Circuit with the jurisdictional vacatur and state-court-remand direction reversed. The district-court judgment is restored against that ground and remains subject to resolution of the five preserved ordinary appellate claims. No disposition of those claims, new trial, fees or sanctions is ordered by this Court.',
sources='[The Sixth Circuit opinion, 68 F.3d 474](https://openjurist.org/68/f3d/474), and [the complete reported record chronology](https://www.law.cornell.edu/supct/html/95-1263.ZO.html) support removal, the preserved motion, pretrial party dismissal and the five remaining issues. The operative premise is actual complete diversity before trial and through judgment. No collusion or manipulation is established, and the separate partial-settlement contention at removal need not be resolved.',
stone="""The approved remedy says 'Reverse and direct reinstatement of the District Court's judgment' and 'The Sixth Circuit shall reinstate the federal jury judgment.' It does not say that the five other preserved appellate claims must be rejected, that ordinary review is barred, or that the verdict is finally affirmed against all challenges. The approved express reservation for independently authorized fees or sanctions further defeats an inference that reinstatement conclusively disposes of every remaining question. The disposition therefore preserves the required restoration by reversing the jurisdictional vacatur and restoring the judgment against that ground, while leaving the separate ordinary appeal pending. Stone joins Parts I–II because his exact approved removal-error, lawful pretrial cure, jurisdiction and collateral-remedy grounds support them. Section II supplies no decision on the five distinct ordinary appellate issues; the standing fallback supplies his vote for the eight-Justice remand disposition on those components only, with no added ground and no Part III join. This neither substitutes a bare final verdict affirmance nor fabricates a dissent from an ordinary appellate process his language does not foreclose. No holding or factual finding of collusion, harmless evidentiary error, proper discovery, or unavailable sanctions is attributed to him.""",
assignment="""The Chief belongs to both final judgment components, including the remaining-issues remand by the approved fallback, and assigns the single coordinated opinion to O'Connor. His lack of a Part III rationale join does not remove him from that judgment coalition. Fit favors O'Connor's Quackenbush authorship on the actual source, stage and effects of remand and her Newman-Green majority join. Scalia and Kennedy require actual pretrial cure rather than appellate manufacture; her opinion can preserve that recorded distinction without expanding their commitments. Ginsburg is a strong statutory-remedy alternative through Things Remembered and Quackenbush; her historical authorship is not an assignment entitlement. Stone's approved separation of authority, procedural error and remedy is also a candidate strength, but his silence on the five appellate issues makes O'Connor better positioned to write the entire coordinated opinion. No expansion is needed once the frozen actual-cure qualification is retained. The preceding-term 101-assignment baseline triggers no concentration restriction. One named Chief assignment to O'Connor results; root integrates the chronological ordinal.""",
joins="""All eight Associates join C1–C3 exactly as frozen: no waiver, no efficiency-created jurisdiction, actual complete diversity before trial, no general harmlessness rule, and all five appellate issues returned. Scalia and Kennedy's Newman-Green dissent concern is answered by a trial-court cure already accomplished, not a new reason attributed to them. Stone joins C1–C2 at the approved level and supports the C3 disposition under the component-specific fallback; no C3 rationale is inferred. The final votes are nine for reversal/restoration against the jurisdictional ground and nine for remand of the independent appellate claims, with an eight-Justice C3 explanation. There is no changed Associate commitment, historical deviation or invented circulation sequence.""",
noncontrol='No separate writing or new substantive noncontrolling proposition issues. Stone\'s judgment-only support for the remaining-issues remand contributes no additional reasoning.',
snapshot='Sections 1332, 1441, 1446 and 1447 supply the actual jurisdiction/removal duties. Finn and Grubbs require careful separation of their facts; Newman-Green retains its own dismissal and prejudice conditions. Willy supplies no merits jurisdiction; Things Remembered and Quackenbush preserve actual covered-remand rules. No pending Robinette opinion or later decision is used.',
uncertainty='Actual pretrial complete diversity is the adopted record premise. No collusion or manipulation is found. The five appellate merits are not established or adjudicated. A materially different assertion that a jurisdictional party remained at trial would require affected-stage revalidation.'
))

